WEBVTT

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:04.262
On this slide, we are focusing on elif handles several categories.

00:00:04.262 --> 00:00:08.967
Start with the meaning of the idea before worrying about the exact syntax.

00:00:08.967 --> 00:00:15.117
Ask what information is going in, what Python is doing with it, and what result should come out.

00:00:15.117 --> 00:00:18.352
Elif supports several possible categories.

00:00:18.352 --> 00:00:25.627
Python checks conditions from top to bottom and stops at the first true branch, so rule order matters.

00:00:25.627 --> 00:00:32.592
In real analytics, this pattern can support thresholds, validation rules, category assignment, alerts,

00:00:32.592 --> 00:00:33.582
and repeated processing.

00:00:33.582 --> 00:00:41.577
The examples are intentionally small so you can trace the logic from the condition to the result.

00:00:41.577 --> 00:00:47.337
Before moving on, explain the main idea in one sentence without reading the slide.

00:00:47.337 --> 00:00:52.754
That quick explanation is a useful check that the concept is becoming reusable knowledge.
